fact WorkItem(id: "WI-860", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 PHASE 8b — the DEFAULTS SUBSTRATE as reflect relations (058 §3.6; the proposal-035 variance pattern, zero new grammar). WORK: (a) a reflect-layer home (sibling section of reflect/typing.anthill, or reflect/instance.anthill): entity DefaultProvider(spec: Symbol, provider: Symbol); rules self_provides(?C, ?S) and default_provider(?S, ?C, ?W) — declared ∪ inferred, consumers read the derived relation only; the declared row derives the CARRIER from the provider's own provision, so CONDITIONALITY COMPOSES FREE (a conditional provision provides only where its chain discharges — ListOrd is default for List[E] exactly where Ordered[E] resolves; assert this as a test). (b) the one_default LOAD check — any two answers coincide — comparing rows at UNIFIABLE carriers: a ground default beside a parametric one for one family is REFUSED (layering defaults by specificity is a boundary-3-style widening needing its own measurement; pin the refusal). NO-DISPLACEMENT NEEDS NO CODE — the inferred row plus one_default refuse an explicit row against a self-providing carrier; assert it as a test, do not write a rule. (c) a DefaultProvider whose provider does not provide the spec is refused (058 §3.5 check 1 re-read as a load check); the provider field is a sort reference in a fact argument (proven surface: fact Covariant(sort: List, param: T), proposal 035). Materialize into a Rust index (the EqDispatchIndex pattern) — the relations are the authority, the hot path reads the index. NOTHING CONSUMES the index yet: rung 2a is phase 8c; this phase is the relations plus the load checks, independently green. ACCEPTANCE: the relations load; one_default refuses a second row naming both declarations; the displacement program is refused BY one_default with the inferred row named; the unifiable ground+parametric pair is refused; the check-1 misuse is refused; the ListOrd conditional-default row loads and is vacuously inert while no rival exists; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: 058 §3.6; design/058-implementation.md §6."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-859"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T22:52:58Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-860", name: "modinst")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-860", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. 058 §3.6's relations live in `stdlib/anthill/reflect/typing.anthill` beside the variance facts they copy — `entity DefaultProvider(spec, provider)`, `provision_carrier/3`, `self_provides/2`, `default_provider/3` (declared ∪ inferred). `one_default` is NOT the constraint the proposal sketched: it compares rows at OVERLAPPING carriers, a question about the row SET and about term overlap that an equality goal over two answers cannot ask, so it is a Rust load check (`kb/defaults.rs`) beside the materialized `DefaultProviderIndex`. Nothing consumes the index — rung 2a is 8c — so every acceptance drives the RELATION or a REFUSAL, never a dispatch.\n\nSELF_PROVIDES IS A COMPARISON, NOT A SECOND COPY. The rule reads a builtin `dispatch_carrier(?P, ?S, ?C)` over `witness_dispatch_carrier_value` — the same owner `provision_supplier` (dispatch) and the WI-838/859 grouping (load) ask — then asks whether that answer IS the provider, both sides normalized through `extract_sort_ref`. So it inherits NONE of the grouping's coherence policy, which is what WI-859's parting instruction required, and cannot drift from the classifier by construction.\n\nTHE FORK WI-859 LEFT, DECIDED: a carrier owning no member DOES self-provide. `self_provides` is over PROVISIONS — who supplies the dictionary — and a carrier whose spec ops all default supplies one made of those defaults. Gating on `carrier_own_op` would make a default row appear and disappear as members are added, and measured, would silently drop no-displacement for exactly the memberless carriers.\n\nONE OWNER HAD TO BECOME CARRIER-NEUTRAL, AND TWO WRONG CUTS BOTH LOADED CLEAN. A `SortProvidesInfo.spec` reads as a `TermId` through `fact_head_named_args` (load) and as a `Value::Node` OCCURRENCE when the resolver matches the same fact (SLD). Cut 1 gated on `Value::Term`: 93 provisions in, ZERO rows out. Cut 2 routed through `unwrap_spec_view_value`, which drops any binding it cannot read as a `TermId`: EVERY WITNESS IN THE TREE classified as a self-provider. Both readings were internally consistent and neither touched a load. The relation-vs-index AGREEMENT TEST is what showed each — it resolves `default_provider(?S,?C,?W)` over the stdlib plus the Rust bindings (90 rows) and compares to the index as SETS. Two derivations of one relation is the WI-838 shape; that test is what keeps them one relation.\n\nTHE PROPOSAL'S OWN WORDING WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED. §3.6 said conditionality composes free because the rule joins the mark with `provides`, which holds only where the chain discharges. The reflect `provides` relation holds UNCONDITIONALLY — WI-1033 says so outright — so `default_provider` answers at `Box[T = E]` for every E. What actually composes is narrower and sufficient: the row carries the carrier AS WRITTEN, so a default can only be consulted for a candidate that already resolved. Proposal amended.\n\nTHE REVIEW FOUND THREE DEFECTS, NOT TIDYING. (1) One wording served three collision shapes and was WRONG for the third: it announced \\\"carrier 'Box[T = Int64]' has 2 default providers\\\" where there are two carriers, and advised a repair already satisfied — `TieRepair`'s exact failure mode, with WI-1012's test hole too (the test checked only that two names appeared). Now `DefaultCollision` with one decision site, decided on the RENDERED carrier so arm and text agree by construction; each arm pinned; collapsing the enum fails 4 tests. (2) `rule DefaultProvider(…) :- …` was a SILENT SKIP — it loads, the relation answers from it, the index and both checks never see it. Measured, then refused as `DerivedDefaultProvider`. (3) The overlap walk was a sixth hand-rolled short-name lookup, label-only, while the SIBLING function had been given the identity-first rung in this same change; `carrier_views_overlap` moved into `typing.rs` and is enrolled in `binding_for_param`'s list. Also: the dedup was coarser than the relation (overlap, not identity) and silently folded a provider's two genuine provisions into one row; and the richer half of both diagnostics sat in `format_at`, which nothing reaches for a post-load pass — measured by running the CLI.\n\nMEASURED CARRIER SHAPES: bare name (`Ref(S)` and the nullary `Fn{S}` both occur) or `Fn{S, named}`. None SortView-wrapped, none with positional args — so no wrapper is peeled and no positional compare exists.\n\nBACK-OUTS, each re-measured after the review: inferred leg → 4 fail; overlap→base-equality → 1; collision enum collapsed → 4; derived-mark refusal → 1; the new `Expr::Apply` display arm → 1 of 2307. Full workspace green (29 binaries, 4288 tests).", at: "2026-08-06T22:53:01Z")
